Q: CS50 - mario pyramid/ the last hash isn&#39;t printed A: <p>To provide some closure to this issue, I analyzed and tested the code as is and experienced the same behavior.  Then, as noted in the good comments, the &quot;for&quot; loop test was refactored to evaluate the range test to &quot;h + h +2&quot;.</p>
<pre><code>    for (int j = 0; j &lt; h + h + 2; j++) /* Refactored to &quot;h + 2&quot; per good comments          */
</code></pre>
<p>Indeed, the missing &quot;#&quot; character is printed and a series of steps going up, a gap of two spaces, and a series of steps going down are printed.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/MarioPyramid/bin/Release$ ./MarioPyramid
Height: 4
   #  #   
  ##  ##  
 ###  ### 
####  ####
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/MarioPyramid/bin/Release$ ./MarioPyramid
Height: 5
    #  #    
   ##  ##   
  ###  ###  
 ####  #### 
#####  #####
</code></pre>
<p>Regarding the concern in the comments about whether or not there is supposed to be a gap, I did a bit of simple refactoring to either allow the gap or ignore having a gap as noted in the following code.</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;
#include &lt;string.h&gt;

#define SIZE 100

int main(int argc, char * argv[])
{
    int h;

    char gap[SIZE];         /* Do not have CS50 installed - using customary input functions     */

    if (argc == 2)
        strcpy(gap, argv[1]);
    else
        strcpy(gap, &quot;NOGAP&quot;);

    printf(&quot;Height: &quot;);
    if (scanf(&quot;%d&quot;, &amp;h) != 1)
        return 1;

    for (int i = 0; i &lt; h; i++)
    {
        for (int j = 0; j &lt; h + h + 2; j++)
        {
            if (h - j - 1 &gt; i || h + h - j + 2 &lt; h - i  )
            {
                printf(&quot; &quot;);
            }
            else if (h == j || h + 1 == j)
            {
                if (strcmp(gap, &quot;GAP&quot;) == 0)
                    printf(&quot; &quot;);
            }
            else if (3*h - j == i)
            {
                printf(&quot;#&quot;);
            }
            else
            {
                printf (&quot;#&quot;);
            }
        }
        printf(&quot;\n&quot;);
    }

    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This refinement allows for the option of printing or not printing a gap via an input parameter.  And just FYI, I do not have the CS50 functionality installed on my virtual machine so standard variable definitions and input functions were used.</p>
<p>Following were a couple of test showing the terminal output created.</p>
<pre><code>craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/MarioPyramid/bin/Release$ ./MarioPyramid NOGAP
Height: 4
   ##   
  ####  
 ###### 
########
craig@Vera:~/C_Programs/Console/MarioPyramid/bin/Release$ ./MarioPyramid GAP
Height: 4
   #  #   
  ##  ##  
 ###  ### 
####  ####
</code></pre>
<p>The takeaway here is to probably delve some more into C tutorials regarding &quot;for&quot; loops and their setup and limit testing.</p>

Q: System.ArgumentNullException: &#39;String reference not set to an instance of a String. Parameter name: s&#39; A: <p>Check <code>value</code> for null (or empty) before calling the rest of the methods:</p>

<pre><code>public static string Hash(string value)
{
    if (String.IsNullOrEmpty(value))
        throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(value));

    var bytes = Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(value);
    var hash = SHA256.Create().ComputeHash(bytes)
    return Convert.ToBase64String(hash);
}
</code></pre>

<p>Having each method's result in its own variable will also help you to debug and better understand the flow.</p>
